# DESCRIPTION
# Perform a hypothesis test for proportions based on the fraction of Americans that make
# new year's resolutions.
# ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Statistics)
## DBchapter(Hypothesis tests)
## DBsection(One sample proportion)
## KEYWORDS('sample proportion', 'hypothesis test')
## Author('Catherine Buell')
## Institution('Fitchburg State')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros(
    "PGstandard.pl",         'PGML.pl',
    'PGstatisticsmacros.pl', "parserPopUp.pl",
    "PGcourse.pl"
);

Context("Numeric")->flags->set(
    tolerance => 0.02,
    tolType   => "absolute",
);

do {
    $pct  = random(15,  25);
    $num  = random(200, 400, 25);
    $s    = int($pct / 100 * $num) + non_zero_random(-10, 10);
    $phat = $s / $num;
    $p0   = $pct / 100;

    $zscore    = ($phat - $p0) / sqrt($p0 * (1 - $p0) / $num);
    $pval      = normal_prob($zscore, 'inf');
    $sig_level = list_random(0.01, 0.05, 0.1, 0.15, 0.20);
} until abs($pval - $sig_level) < 0.1;

@opts   = ('\(<\)', '\(>\)', '\(=\)', '\(\neq\)');
$popup1 = DropDown([@opts], 2, useHTMLSelect => 0);
$popup2 = DropDown([@opts], 1, useHTMLSelect => 0);
$popup3 = DropDown([ "Left-tailed", "Right-tailed", "Two-tailed" ], 1);
$popup4 = DropDown(
    [
        "We will reject the null hypothesis.",
        "We fail to reject the null hypothesis."
    ],
    $pval < $sig_level ? 0 : 1
);

BEGIN_PGML
An article claims that [$pct]% of British people make New Year’s resolutions. 
A social scientist performs a hypothesis test to determine if the percentage 
is higher among Americans. She surveys [$num]
American adults and finds that [$s] of them make New Year’s resolutions. 
For the hypothesis test, use a [$sig_level*100]% level of significance.

a) The null hypothesis would be: [`H_0`]: p [_]{$popup1} [____________]{Real($p0)}

b) The alternative hypothesis would be: [`H_a`]:  p [_]{$popup2}   [____________]{Real($pct/100)}

c) What type of test is being asked? [_]{$popup3}

d) What is the alpha level? [`\alpha`] = [__]{Real($sig_level)}{10}

e) What is [`n`]? [_]{Real($num)}{10}

f) What is [`\hat{p}`] (to two decimal places)? [____________]{Real($s/$num)}

g) If the [`p`]-value is [@ Round($pval,3) @], what should be our conclusion? [_]{$popup4}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
a) Since we are testing whether or not [$pct]% of all new roller coaster are family friendly, the null hypothesis would be [` H_0: \; p = [$p0] `].
b) In the problem, the enthusiast believes that number is low, so the alternative is the opposite of this or [`H_a: \; p > [$p0] `]. 
c) Since in the alternative hypothesis there is a greater than, this is a *right-tailed test*. 
d) The [`\alpha`]-level is the level of significance as a fraction or [$sig_level]. 
e) The value of [`n`] is the number of samples or in this case [$num].
f) The sample proportion [`\hat{p}`] is the fraction of "successes" or in this case, the number of family roller coasters or [`[$s]/[$num] = [@ Round($s/$num,2) @]`].
g) Recall that we reject the null hypothesis only when the [`p`]-value is less than [`\alpha`] and in this case it [@ $pval < $sig_level ? 'is' : 'is not' @], therefore:  [$popup4] 
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
